1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,660
One in five hundred people has this disease.

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:08,890
A test catches ninety-nine percent of cases and wrongly flags one percent of the healthy.

3
00:00:09,270 --> 00:00:12,240
If yours says positive, what's the chance you're sick?

4
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:13,510
Pick a number.

5
00:00:15,466 --> 00:00:20,016
Most doctors and medical students given a problem like this got it wrong.

6
00:00:20,366 --> 00:00:27,346
Their most common answer was the test's own accuracy, here ninety-nine percent: you'd start treatment today.

7
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:29,430
Check that rule by counting.

8
00:00:29,630 --> 00:00:33,460
Here are ten thousand people, and suppose half of them are sick.

9
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:34,930
Test everyone.

10
00:00:35,130 --> 00:00:39,360
Ninety-nine percent of the sick light up, and one percent of the healthy.

11
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:43,520
Of the five thousand who lit up, ninety-nine percent are sick.

12
00:00:43,820 --> 00:00:45,300
Here, the rule holds.

13
00:00:45,666 --> 00:00:49,666
Now make the disease rare, like yours: one in five hundred.

14
00:00:49,866 --> 00:00:51,776
That leaves twenty sick people.

15
00:00:52,133 --> 00:00:53,473
Run the same test.

16
00:00:53,673 --> 00:01:00,133
About twenty sick people light up, plus one percent of the nine thousand nine hundred eighty healthy ones.

17
00:01:00,333 --> 00:01:01,783
That's about a hundred.

18
00:01:02,133 --> 00:01:05,883
So of a hundred and twenty positives, only twenty are sick.

19
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:07,590
Seventeen percent.

20
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:10,330
The rule said ninety-nine.

21
00:01:10,530 --> 00:01:12,110
Six times too high.

22
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:17,710
One percent sounds small, but here it's one percent of nearly everyone.

23
00:01:18,066 --> 00:01:22,616
Your positive result ruled out everyone who tested negative.

24
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:28,310
What's left is a smaller sample space: these hundred and twenty people.

25
00:01:28,666 --> 00:01:32,026
Your chance of being sick is the red share of this crowd.

26
00:01:32,326 --> 00:01:34,726
Twenty, out of twenty plus a hundred.

27
00:01:36,333 --> 00:01:41,113
Unrounded, the red count is twenty sick people times ninety-nine percent.

28
00:01:41,713 --> 00:01:47,263
The blue count is nine thousand nine hundred eighty healthy people times one percent.

29
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:51,380
Divide top and bottom by the ten thousand we started with.

30
00:01:51,580 --> 00:01:59,560
Twenty people become two in a thousand, and nine thousand nine hundred eighty become nine hundred ninety-eight in a thousand.

31
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:01,150
The share can't change.

32
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:07,930
These are how common each group is, and these, how often each group tests positive: Bayes' theorem.

33
00:02:08,266 --> 00:02:13,556
All it does is shrink the sample space to what the evidence allows, then take the share.

34
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:17,180
Swap the disease for a police watch list.

35
00:02:17,533 --> 00:02:24,353
At the twenty seventeen Champions League final, about a hundred and seventy thousand people were in Cardiff.

36
00:02:24,553 --> 00:02:32,103
The watch lists held about twelve hundred faces, so even a small error rate per face would swamp the real matches.

37
00:02:32,466 --> 00:02:35,156
Face-recognition cameras scanned the crowd.

38
00:02:35,456 --> 00:02:38,876
They raised two thousand four hundred and seventy alerts.

39
00:02:39,266 --> 00:02:41,896
Shrink to the alerts, and take the red share.

40
00:02:42,276 --> 00:02:46,786
Police counted a hundred and seventy-three as right: seven percent.

41
00:02:47,133 --> 00:02:51,483
In machine learning, the share of alerts that are right is called precision.

42
00:02:51,833 --> 00:02:55,363
The university's evaluators counted three percent here.

43
00:02:55,743 --> 00:03:02,683
After a new algorithm and better photos cut the false alarms, they counted forty-six at the Six Nations.

44
00:03:03,033 --> 00:03:04,253
Back to your test.

45
00:03:05,153 --> 00:03:08,393
The title's ninety-nine percent accuracy is real.

46
00:03:08,773 --> 00:03:15,093
But a test that calls everyone healthy scores ninety-nine point eight: more accurate, and useless.

47
00:03:15,433 --> 00:03:18,663
Your seventeen percent assumes a random screen.

48
00:03:18,963 --> 00:03:29,003
Symptoms raise the one in five hundred, and the answer climbs: at one in a hundred, fifty percent; at one in two, ninety-nine, where the rule held.

49
00:03:31,033 --> 00:03:36,143
Ninety-nine percent is the test's recall: how often the sick test positive.

50
00:03:36,443 --> 00:03:41,263
Seventeen percent is its precision: how often the positive are sick.

